    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The French formed each of five divisions into hollow rectangles, with cavalry and baggage in the centre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> at the corners, and these squares withstood repeated cavalry charges.</li><li>Murad Bey's plan was to hold the French on his fortified flanks, at Embabeh and Biktil, and strike their centre with cavalry, but Bon's division stormed Embabeh near the river.</li><li>The victory opened the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and marked the beginning of the end of seven centuries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> dominance, but its strategic effect was short-lived once Nelson destroyed the French fleet ten days later.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Bonaparte named the battle after the Pyramids, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza">Great Pyramid of Giza</a> was distant from the field. In his order of the day he told his men that from the top of those pyramids forty centuries were contemplating them.</p></aside>
    </section>

